= Three cells where the method reaches its limit — stated plainly


The tightest-government method is only as complete as the world's regulators. Three cells cannot be
set by lookup, and this standard says so rather than inventing a number:


+ *Mercury (all subcategories) — no government sets an infant-formula limit.* The nearest limit in the world is China's 20 µg/kg for _canned complementary foods_, a different product. There is nothing to adopt. Mercury is therefore *monitored, not limited*: every certification lot is screened for total mercury, with reflex methylmercury speciation on any detection, but no numeric pass/fail limit is published where no government has set one. This is the honest output of the method, not an omission.
+ *Hexavalent chromium — unregulated in food worldwide.* No government sets a Cr-VI food limit (the only Cr-VI limits are for drinking water). Total chromium has one applicable government number — China's 2,000 µg/kg for milk powder (a broad dairy category that covers cow-milk formula powder), shown with a ᵈ flag as a fallback, not a formula-specific limit. Cr-VI itself is *monitored, not limited*, with a 100 µg/kg total-chromium manufacturing-anomaly screen that triggers speciation.
+ *Tin — regulated, but on an uncertain basis.* Only India sets a non-canned infant-formula tin limit (5,000 µg/kg); every other government regulates tin only for _canned_ product (50,000 µg/kg, a container-migration limit). The India value's basis is unresolved in our register (the secondary source records it as-consumed; the FSSAI 2011 primary text records it as-sold — a 7× difference). The tin cell is therefore *provisional* pending confirmation against the primary Indian instrument; canned formula is held to the 50,000 µg/kg world consensus.


Everything else in this standard is a firm, citable government maximum level.



